DISTINCT SUBSEQUENCES
🔹 Problem Statement

Given two strings s and t, return the number of distinct subsequences of s that equal t.

A subsequence is obtained by deleting some (or no) characters without changing order.

Example 1
Input: s = "rabbbit", t = "rabbit"
Output: 3

Example 2
Input: s = "babgbag", t = "bag"
Output: 5

🔹 Why this problem is 💀 but valuable

✔ Classic Dynamic Programming
✔ Tests understanding of string DP transitions
✔ Very common Google-level DP question
✔ Forces you to reason, not memorize

🔹 Core Idea 💡

Let:

dp[i][j] = number of ways first i characters of s
           can form first j characters of t

🔹 Transition Logic
Case 1: Characters match
s[i-1] == t[j-1]
dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]  (take char)
         + dp[i-1][j]    (skip char)

Case 2: Characters don't match
dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j]    (skip s character)

🔹 Base Cases
Condition	Value
t is empty	1 way
s is empty & t not empty	0 ways
🔹 DP Table Example 🧠
s = "rabbbit", t = "rabbit"
      r  a  b  b  i  t
   1  0  0  0  0  0  0
r  1  1  0  0  0  0  0
a  1  1  1  0  0  0  0
b  1  1  1  1  0  0  0
b  1  1  1  2  1  0  0
b  1  1  1  3  3  1  0
i  1  1  1  3  3  3  0
t  1  1  1  3  3  3  3


Answer = 3

🔹 Java Code (2D DP – clear & interview safe)
class Solution {
    public int numDistinct(String s, String t) {

        int m = s.length();
        int n = t.length();

        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        // Empty target string
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
            dp[i][0] = 1;
        }

        // Fill DP table
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {

                if (s.charAt(i - 1) == t.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + dp[i - 1][j];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[m][n];
    }
}

🔹 Time & Space Complexity
Metric	Complexity
Time	O(M × N)
Space	O(M × N)
